{"full":"hf:VIDraft/ai-world","spaceId":"VIDraft/ai-world","name":"CIVOS: Emergence or Recitation?","source":"huggingface","description":"Do LLM agents discover, or recite memorized history?","category":"AI & machine learning","language":null,"topics":["docker","llm-agents","multi-agent","agent-simulation","emergent-behavior","memorization","data-contamination","evaluation","benchmark","ablation","world-model","region:us"],"technologies":[],"likes":47,"created":"2026-08-30T09:27:59.000Z","updated":"2026-09-05T01:40:06.000Z","license":null,"runtime":"RUNNING","appUrl":"https://vidraft-ai-world.hf.space/","demo":"https://huggingface.co/spaces/VIDraft/ai-world","availability":"available","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-06T05:40:21.635Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-06T05:40:21.635Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-06T05:40:21.635Z","color":"#3c3250","ink":"#e4d4ff","symbol":"🌍","overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["Multi-agent studies routinely report that a society \"emerged\" or that a civilisation \"developed\". The language models driving those agents, however, have already been trained on human history: they know that striking stone yields an edge, that twisted fibre yields cord, and that fire precedes agriculture precedes metallurgy. Any observed trajectory is therefore consistent with two incompatible accounts — genuine exploration, or replay of memorised history — and we are aware of no prior study that includes a control capable of separating them.","CIVOS is that control. It runs the same task under conditions that differ only in what the model's prior knowledge can do for it, while the task's structure is held fixed. If prior knowledge is doing the work, performance must fall when that knowledge is made unusable. If exploration is doing the work, it must not.","Across 600 paired runs on two model families we find that making prior knowledge unusable collapses discovery — reproduced on both families, with a larger effect on the second. A second finding, that wrong prior knowledge is worse than none, replicated under two independent manipulations within the first family but did not replicate on the second; we report it as possibly model-dependent rather than general. A third comparison — whether the knowledge-free condition beats a random floor — is not established on either family. We report that last one because we initially concluded the opposite,…"],"features":[],"sourceUrl":"https://huggingface.co/spaces/VIDraft/ai-world/blob/main/README.md","fingerprint":"c4dfb4050e0d6405e8e70b7bbb24a0dbcd64f20b170fe68d584eb94357fb3838","extractedAt":"2026-10-06T05:40:21.662Z"},"overviewCheckedAt":"2026-10-06T05:40:21.662Z","projectInsights":{"version":1,"whatItDoes":{"text":"Multi-agent studies routinely report that a society \"emerged\" or that a civilisation \"developed\". The language models driving those agents, however, have already been trained on human history: they know that striking stone yields an edge, that twisted fibre yields cord, and that fire precedes agriculture precedes metallurgy. Any observed trajectory is therefore consistent with two incompatible accounts — genuine exploration, or replay of memorised history — and we are aware of no prior study that includes a control capable of separating them.","sourceUrl":"https://huggingface.co/spaces/VIDraft/ai-world/blob/main/README.md"},"audience":[],"requirements":[],"fingerprint":"c4dfb4050e0d6405e8e70b7bbb24a0dbcd64f20b170fe68d584eb94357fb3838","sourceUrl":"https://huggingface.co/spaces/VIDraft/ai-world/blob/main/README.md","checkedAt":"2026-10-06T05:40:21.662Z"},"overviewRepoUpdated":"2026-09-05T01:40:06.000Z","readmeSnapshot":{"version":1,"status":"saved","sourceUrl":"https://huggingface.co/spaces/VIDraft/ai-world/blob/main/README.md","checkedAt":"2026-10-06T05:40:21.662Z","hash":"c4dfb4050e0d6405e8e70b7bbb24a0dbcd64f20b170fe68d584eb94357fb3838","originalBytes":12003,"storedBytes":12003,"truncated":false,"searchTerms":["knowledge","model","what","world","runs","c